# Build context for the fuel-monitor image.
#
# ALLOW-LIST, not a deny-list, and that shape is the point. `COPY . .` with no
# .dockerignore at all baked `/app/.env` -- BLUELINK_USERNAME, BLUELINK_PASSWORD,
# BLUELINK_PIN and the ntfy credentials, 460 bytes -- into an image layer on
# plexpi, dated Apr 10 2026 and surviving every rebuild since. docker-compose.yml
# mounts the real `.env` over it at runtime, so nothing ever surfaced it; it is
# readable with `docker save` or `docker history` regardless, and would travel
# with the image to any registry.
#
# A deny-list fails open: the next secret added at the repo root ships unless
# somebody remembers to add a line here. This denies everything and re-admits
# only what the RUNTIME needs, so the default for a new file at the repo root is
# "not in the image". The cost is that a new runtime entrypoint must be
# re-admitted below -- `__tests__/docker-image.spec.ts` derives the required set
# from the tracked crontab and entrypoint, so forgetting turns that suite red
# rather than the hourly cron job.
#
# READ THE SCOPE OF THAT GUARANTEE CAREFULLY, because an earlier draft of this
# file overstated it and shipped. **Fail-closed holds at the repo root and
# inside `deployment/`. It does NOT hold inside `src/`.** `!src` re-admits the
# whole library -- there is no enumerating 40-odd files that change every time
# someone adds a region -- so a new file anywhere under `src/` ships by default.
# The trailing `**/*.env` block below is what stops the secrets-shaped half of
# that; `src/credentials.json` would still ship, and nothing here can see it.
# Measured against real BuildKit, not reasoned about: with `!deployment` as a
# whole directory (the shape this file had until it was narrowed below),
# `deployment/plexpi-secret.yaml` and `deployment/creds.env` both shipped.
#
# Excluding node_modules is load-bearing, not hygiene: the runtime stage copies
# a dev-free tree from the `deps` stage and `COPY . .` runs AFTER it, so a host
# node_modules in the context would overwrite it with whatever the box happens
# to have installed. The box has none today, which is luck -- `npm ci` in the
# checkout is one documented troubleshooting step away.
*

# Dependency manifests: `npm ci` needs BOTH, and refuses if they disagree.
!package.json
!package-lock.json

# tsx reads this to resolve paths and compiler options.
!tsconfig.json

# Runtime entrypoints invoked by deployment/crontab and deployment/entrypoint.sh.
!monitor.ts
!monitor-fuel.ts
!status-json.ts
!get-fuel-level.ts
!alert-backends.ts

# The library itself. monitor.ts imports './src/index.ts' directly -- it does
# NOT read dist/, which is why the image no longer builds one.
!src

# crontab + entrypoint.sh, NAMED INDIVIDUALLY rather than as `!deployment`.
#
# An earlier draft took the whole directory, on the stated grounds that per-file
# re-inclusion under an excluded parent "relies on a BuildKit implementation
# detail rather than something documented". That reason is wrong, and it was
# checked rather than argued: a context built with `*` + `!deployment/crontab` +
# `!deployment/entrypoint.sh` on docker 29.4 admits exactly those two files. It
# is the documented `!` exception syntax, not a detail.
#
# The broad form was also not free. It shipped `deployment/README.md`, and it
# meant any future `deployment/<anything>` -- a secret, a vault file, a
# host-specific override -- would ship by default, which is the exact failure
# this file's header claims to prevent.
!deployment/crontab
!deployment/entrypoint.sh

# Secrets, last so no re-inclusion above can reach them. The root-level pair is
# redundant against `*` and kept anyway, because the cost of being wrong here is
# credentials in a published layer.
#
# `**/*.env` is NOT redundant and is the one line here that was missing. `**/`
# means "zero or more directory levels", so `**/.env` matches `.env` and
# `deployment/.env` and nothing else -- it does not match a file whose name
# merely ENDS in `.env`. Verified against real BuildKit: without the line below,
# `src/a.env` ships.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
